aren't I, I don't know.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:39.830
- Well, you're not Stacey.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:43.790
I mean, I meet people on
social media all the time,

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:46.150
I don't always meet CEOs on social media.

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:50.720
So when I met a black woman
CEO, from March of Dimes,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:53.390
I was like, "What? Let me find out."

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:55.560
So I wasn't gonna say it Stacey.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.863
That's why I allowed you you'd
answer the question first

00:11:57.863 --> 00:12:00.830
because I wasn't sure what
we were actually gonna say.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:05.250
But, yes, Stacey and I
met on social media and I-

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.362
- She slid in my Dms, but not in...

00:12:07.362 --> 00:12:08.195
In a good way.
(all laughing)

00:12:08.195 --> 00:12:10.890
I admit I did slide in Stacey's Dms

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:14.830
and I was so honored that she replied

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:17.870
and that I felt a connection.

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:21.270
And I came to the March for Moms

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:23.870
and I usually participate every year

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:25.970
and speak at their rally
on the National Mall

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:27.830
and participate in their advocacy day.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.290
But Stacey was like, "We
have to go to dinner.

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:31.410
I want to take you to dinner."

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:35.660
We had dinner and we put on
lipstick, we took our selfies.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:38.220
And I was just like, I felt so connected,

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:43.220
but also left feeling like
I want to be in partnership

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:46.380
with March of Dimes because of Stacey.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:50.210
So I've been ringing the
bells and advocating.

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:53.470
I mean, even when we're not
technically in partnership,

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:56.950
like we are right now in the
#postpartumrevolutionroadtrip,

00:12:56.950 --> 00:12:59.950
I make sure at all of my events,

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:03.050
I have March of Dimes
resources for my moms

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:05.560
and for folks that come to my events,

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:07.640
but aren't even expecting
to be lectured about

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:09.110
postpartum depression, I'm like, "Hold on,

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:11.150
here, take a March of Dimes pamphlet.

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:12.500
Here's some resources for you."

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.840
I make sure that I lean into
the work that they're doing

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:21.250
with all of my events and
all of my work that I do.

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:22.453
- Yeah. Well, that's great.
- She's amazing.

00:13:22.453 --> 00:13:24.270
- I think that speaks to both,

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.530
the authenticity that both of you have,

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:28.300
just in your interactions with each other

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:29.340
and just how you come across,

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:31.780
it seems like you really fired off of that

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:33.680
when you first interacted to first met

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.070
and have that common cause too.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:36.810
So it's really exciting to hear, I mean,

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:38.720
all the important work that you're doing,

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.840
you both alluded to some
of the pushback though,

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:43.300
that you got early on and I would,

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:45.850
or people not believing in the mission.

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:49.030
I'm just curious if you could
elaborate on why that is,

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:51.670
especially in this day and age,

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.200
when there seems to be
more conversation now,

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:55.234
but there's always still that pushback.

00:13:55.234 --> 00:13:56.067
- Yeah.
- Where do you think

00:13:56.067 --> 00:13:57.024
that's coming from?

00:13:57.024 --> 00:14:00.210
- Well, I'll start Angelina, I think...

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:03.378
So there's pushback at a
number of different things.

00:14:03.378 --> 00:14:06.977
I mean, I think first
of all, in the country,

00:14:06.977 --> 00:14:09.840
we weren't even having
these conversations around

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:12.330
maternal health, generally,

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.847
physical, mental, any kind of, women,

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:20.523
because I think a lot of it is
that women generally are not

00:14:20.523 --> 00:14:24.423
prioritized in this country
the way we should be.

00:14:24.423 --> 00:14:27.100
And then certainly pregnant women are not,

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:32.100
it's appalling though that
in a society like ours,

00:14:32.499 --> 00:14:36.109
we wouldn't be putting as much priority on

00:14:36.109 --> 00:14:38.073
the lives of moms and babies.

00:14:38.073 --> 00:14:40.570
I mean, it's just striking.

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:44.080
And we, obviously we talked
a lot about the fact that,

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.104
we are considered the most
dangerous developed nation in

00:14:46.104 --> 00:14:48.460
the world in which to give birth now,

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:52.470
how in the U S do we find
that at any acceptable, right?

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:57.150
And so I think it's just been, I honestly,

00:14:57.150 --> 00:15:00.370
I think what's happening
is that the storytelling is

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:02.120
beginning to breakthrough. I mean,

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:06.460
Angelina telling her a story,
Beyonce telling her stories,

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:07.620
Serena telling her story,

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.620
or someone like Charles
Johnson telling his story,

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:13.623
about Kira who died, in a hospital in LA.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:17.180
Once those stories...

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:18.860
Storytelling we know is very powerful.

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.330
So March of Dimes can be
ringing the bell all day.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:22.720
And we have been talking
about premature birth

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.040
for a long time, but when those acts,

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:26.380
when those stories
started to break through,

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.930
it changes the whole narrative,

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:30.366
it changes the public awareness

00:15:30.366 --> 00:15:34.320
in sort of embeds itself
in everyday conversation

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.963
and people's consciousness in a way that

00:15:36.963 --> 00:15:40.860
organizations like mine can't always do.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:44.319
And so, now we've gotten to
the point where, I don't,

00:15:44.319 --> 00:15:46.660
I still don't think we're
paying enough attention to it,

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.110
I still don't think we're
prioritizing it in a way that it

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:51.963
should be in dealing with it
with the sense of urgency.

00:15:51.963 --> 00:15:54.260
But I definitely can say that we're at

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:55.980
a different level of
conversation about it,

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:58.813
and a lot of different
level of attentiveness.

00:15:58.813 --> 00:16:01.770
And I'm saying that from the
respect of Washington DC,

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:03.560
where I think policy makers are trying to

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:05.800
are starting to look at
this very differently.

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:06.780
So it just takes time.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:09.559
These things don't always
happen overnight, but we're,

00:16:09.559 --> 00:16:12.273
we certainly have made a lot
of progress in my short time

00:16:12.273 --> 00:16:15.704
here at the March of Dimes
which is 4 1/2 years.

00:16:15.704 --> 00:16:17.413
Then Angelina, what do you think?

00:16:19.307 --> 00:16:24.150
- I realized that I have
a unique voice, in that,

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:29.150
I am yes a storyteller, but
I'm also a volatile comedian.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:33.521
Okay and so that's where
the pushback for me came,

00:16:33.521 --> 00:16:38.440
with people particularly organizations,

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:39.990
not in the comedic world.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:44.160
I mean, I've gotten pushed back
on both sides, both in the,

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:48.011
on the organizational side and
on the Hollywood side. Okay.

00:16:48.011 --> 00:16:52.492
So on, and I say, organizational,
I mean like non-profits,

00:16:52.492 --> 00:16:56.270
policies, hospitals, brands, et cetera,

00:16:56.270 --> 00:17:00.128
On that side, there's
pushback or hesitancy

00:17:00.128 --> 00:17:05.128
because I am a comedian and I
can say whatever it is I want.

00:17:05.181 --> 00:17:08.375
And usually comics go
off the rails sometimes,

00:17:08.375 --> 00:17:12.392
they can go off the rails and
say crazy things, et cetera.

00:17:12.392 --> 00:17:14.000
So I'm a liability.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.623
I can be perceived as a
liability on that side.

00:17:18.478 --> 00:17:22.217
For example, I have
been fundraising for my,

00:17:22.217 --> 00:17:24.140
"The Push for Permission" documentary film

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:28.520
that I'm currently crowdsourcing
and, putting together

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:31.077
and producing and I went to a hospital

00:17:31.077 --> 00:17:34.145
and we had an amazing pitch meeting.

00:17:34.145 --> 00:17:36.680
We showed them all of our assets,

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:38.470
all of the production companies

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.880
and networks that are
interested in our film.

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:41.830
And they're like, "Well,

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:43.680
we don't know what you're gonna say."

00:17:44.683 --> 00:17:48.083
So there was hesitancy from that.

00:17:48.083 --> 00:17:51.639
And, I'm an artist first.
I don't wanna be censored,

00:17:51.639 --> 00:17:55.990
but I also realize their
obligation to their board

00:17:55.990 --> 00:18:00.596
and those that keep the
organization functioning.

00:18:00.596 --> 00:18:02.170
So that's okay.

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:06.150
What troubles me the
most, I would say is that

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:13.750
Hollywood has pushed back in
a way that reinforces the idea

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:22.380
that you don't matter
unless you're a celebrity,

00:18:22.490 --> 00:18:27.490
you don't matter because quote,
no one knows who you are.

00:18:29.484 --> 00:18:33.510
And I've had a number
of production companies

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:36.625
and networks tell me, "Well,

00:18:36.625 --> 00:18:40.070
why do we care that you
have postpartum depression?

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:44.710
We could ask Gwyneth
Paltrow. We could ask-"

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:46.430
- Basically Megan or somebody. Yeah.

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:47.400
- Exactly.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:50.696
And regardless of my messaging,

00:18:50.696 --> 00:18:54.610
my messaging is very
different, from a Beyonce,

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:57.262
definitely from a Gwyneth
Paltrow coz I'm sister

00:18:57.262 --> 00:19:01.080
and I have a comedic voice, so
there's gonna be some levity.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:02.360
There's gonna be humor.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:06.200
There's gonna be
accessibility to my messaging.

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.554
But in Hollywood, it doesn't
matter because quote unquote,

00:19:09.554 --> 00:19:11.238
no one knows who you are.

00:19:11.238 --> 00:19:15.641
So I, that's why I have
just been so hyper-focused

00:19:15.641 --> 00:19:20.270
on my small donors and knocking on doors

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:23.270
and I'm just going to make the film,

00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:26.139
make the project that I
wanna see in the world.

00:19:26.139 --> 00:19:29.621
That's not triggering, that
moms can see themselves in,

00:19:29.621 --> 00:19:33.425
and I've gained partnerships
from organizations along

00:19:33.425 --> 00:19:36.712
the way, Hollywood is slow
going, but it doesn't matter

00:19:36.712 --> 00:19:39.383
because I know that there's
an audience for my work.

00:19:39.383 --> 00:19:42.540
And I know that there's value in it,

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:44.180
whether or not I'm a celebrity.

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:46.380
- Yeah. Yeah. Wow.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:48.300
I appreciate you sharing
that level of candor too.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:52.263
Cause I mean, so speaking as
a complete Hollywood outsider,

00:19:52.263 --> 00:19:55.860
just hearing you talk about
that just makes it sound like

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:58.830
that the, there isn't an
emphasis on the message.

00:19:58.830 --> 00:19:59.913
- Never.
- Whatsoever.

00:19:59.913 --> 00:20:03.078
- There's emphasis on
the money and I get it.

00:20:03.078 --> 00:20:05.067
Listen, which is why I'm like,

00:20:05.067 --> 00:20:08.667
"I will raise my own money to
get my own message out there."

00:20:08.667 --> 00:20:11.110
Because I know that
there's an audience for it

00:20:11.110 --> 00:20:15.220
and if its slow going,
whatever it is, we're doing it.

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:18.105
So, it's in motion, it's happening.

00:20:18.105 --> 00:20:21.926
And moms are reaching out to
me in droves on social media

00:20:21.926 --> 00:20:25.858
and organizations, the University
of Nairobi reached out.

00:20:25.858 --> 00:20:29.640
I started working with WHO,
University of South Africa,

00:20:29.640 --> 00:20:32.260
the Israeli government reached
out and wants me to come

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:35.040
and do trainings, and
with Doulas and Burke,

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:37.320
Hollywood is not the end ordeal.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:40.835
My mission is bigger than the nose.

00:20:40.835 --> 00:20:45.355
It's fully for the moms who are
in distress, the moms to be.

00:20:45.355 --> 00:20:47.406
So I'm clear on my audience.

00:20:47.406 --> 00:20:49.432
If Hollywood wants to be
late, let them be late.

00:20:49.432 --> 00:20:50.400
That's all right.

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:52.700
- Yep. And the way you've
talked about that too,

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:54.572
it sounds like people are able to relate

00:20:54.572 --> 00:20:56.879
and that might be an
advantage of the non Hollywood

00:20:56.879 --> 00:21:00.494
route anyway, that people
can see you for who you are

00:21:00.494 --> 00:21:03.430
not have to worry about stature

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:04.760
and not to say that
you're not accomplished,

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:06.710
but just say, "Okay, this
is someone I can relate to.

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:08.830
They've gone through it.
I can connect to them."

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:11.820
And I think there's just such
a power that comes with that.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:12.690
- I agree.

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:16.735
And I joke and I say,
nobody knows who I am.

00:21:16.735 --> 00:21:21.430
So people care more,
and I have less to lose.

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:24.520
So I can talk about, my broken lady parts

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:27.260
and how much I hated my husband
after I had my daughter,

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:29.742
because I'm not Serena Williams.

00:21:29.742 --> 00:21:32.709
If Serena says that she has endorsements.

00:21:32.709 --> 00:21:34.493
- She's gonna break the internet, right?

00:21:34.493 --> 00:21:36.257
- That's right she's
gonna break the internet

00:21:36.257 --> 00:21:38.547
and then that overshadows text capability-

00:21:38.547 --> 00:21:39.380
- Yeah, the message, the message.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:40.999
Yeah.
- Yes the message.

00:21:40.999 --> 00:21:44.160
And me, nobody knows me, so
girl, you gonna get the whole,

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:47.390
prospect of my business
and that makes it better,

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:48.223
more accessible.

00:21:48.223 --> 00:21:50.180
- But I also think that the...

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:51.740
So let's get to the message, right?

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:54.220
Which is that, I mean,

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:56.635
how many people really do want
to talk about mental health

00:21:56.635 --> 00:21:59.114
in a really sincere way anyway?

00:21:59.114 --> 00:22:01.858
How many people actually want
to hear about it in a way

00:22:01.858 --> 00:22:06.550
that is not uncomfortable for them?

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:11.550
I mean, it's just, we have,
we are so incapable right now,

00:22:15.644 --> 00:22:20.300
incapable of really
acknowledging the degree to which

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:24.403
we completely under fund, under resource,

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:27.710
the mental health challenges

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:30.062
that so many people in this country face.

00:22:30.062 --> 00:22:33.786
I mean, we're talking about
women who are pregnant

00:22:33.786 --> 00:22:36.706
or who give birth and who are new moms,

00:22:36.706 --> 00:22:39.180
but frankly, I mean, what, where,

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:40.748
we wouldn't even have an opioid crisis

00:22:40.748 --> 00:22:42.470
to the extent that we do,

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:45.000
if we didn't have a lot
of people struggling

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:50.000
with their mental health
and this is not new, right?

00:22:50.222 --> 00:22:53.523
Like we've been dealing
with this for a long time.

00:22:53.523 --> 00:22:55.980
And I think it's just
really something that

00:22:56.930 --> 00:23:00.616
we are trying to get this message.

00:23:00.616 --> 00:23:03.553
I mean, it's, so to the
question about pushback, I mean,

00:23:03.553 --> 00:23:07.400
I think a big reason why we
do see the pushback is that

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:10.040
people are just really, really
struggling with even how

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:13.278
to acknowledge these
issues and talk about them

00:23:13.278 --> 00:23:15.830
and then frankly deal with them.

00:23:15.830 --> 00:23:18.973
And I do think that's why
it's so important that people,

00:23:18.973 --> 00:23:23.888
even if Angelina is whatever
level of celebrity she wants

00:23:23.888 --> 00:23:27.948
to call herself, she still
has a huge following.

00:23:27.948 --> 00:23:30.511
She has a huge voice.

00:23:30.511 --> 00:23:33.598
And I do think that it
makes a big difference

00:23:33.598 --> 00:23:37.620
when people who are visible,
who are in the public side,

00:23:37.620 --> 00:23:40.948
make themselves vulnerable
to talk about these things,

00:23:40.948 --> 00:23:45.403
because it helps all of
us when I go to the hill,

00:23:45.403 --> 00:23:48.170
if it helps me have a member of Congress,

00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:50.210
has seen something on social media

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:52.220
or a movie or a documentary,

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:55.747
like then it makes it that
much easier when we had to say,

00:23:55.747 --> 00:23:57.550
"Yeah, well, you know about that now,"

00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:59.922
go ahead and give like however
many millions of dollars

00:23:59.922 --> 00:24:02.743
(laughing) we need to
make sure that we have

00:24:02.743 --> 00:24:05.278
a 24 hour mental health
hotline for new moms

00:24:05.278 --> 00:24:07.710
or to make sure we have the resources

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:08.970
to make sure we have Medicaid coverage

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:12.460
for women who are seeking
those kinds of supports

00:24:12.460 --> 00:24:14.667
and need their coverage paid for, right.

00:24:14.667 --> 00:24:16.570
That it makes it that much easier.

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:18.760
So it's all of these individual efforts

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:21.424
that collectively roll
up to something where

00:24:21.424 --> 00:24:23.211
we actually eventually have a movement

00:24:23.211 --> 00:24:24.930
that actually moves the needle

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.680
and then it gets change to a
place where we can get help to

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:28.990
the people that really need it.

00:24:28.990 --> 00:24:30.350
- Yeah, I think that's so all sad.

00:24:30.350 --> 00:24:31.353
And I'm so, so glad that you're able

00:24:31.353 --> 00:24:33.939
to harness the power of
all of those components,

00:24:33.939 --> 00:24:34.772
even when you're on the hill.

00:24:34.772 --> 00:24:37.090
Cause I mean, that
makes a huge difference.

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:38.330
And I wanted to pick
up on one of the things

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:39.163
that you mentioned too,

00:24:39.163 --> 00:24:42.111
just in terms of us not
knowing how to really engage

00:24:42.111 --> 00:24:44.568
and talk about it, because
even as you mentioned,

00:24:44.568 --> 00:24:46.260
as I'm sure you all know the stats too.

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:48.656
I mean, even before
COVID, just thinking about

00:24:48.656 --> 00:24:51.650
mental health challenges in
this country, one in five,

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:53.970
who are struggling with mental
health in some shape or form.

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:56.501
So when you break that down,
most of us, either personally,

00:24:56.501 --> 00:24:59.295
ourselves or family members
or friends know somebody

00:24:59.295 --> 00:25:02.094
who's struggling in some shape
or form with mental health,

00:25:02.094 --> 00:25:05.218
but we still don't know how
to engage with it always.

00:25:05.218 --> 00:25:07.550
And we still don't fully acknowledge it.

00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:08.690
So I'm even thinking about,

00:25:08.690 --> 00:25:09.523
as you're talking about on the hill,

00:25:09.523 --> 00:25:12.064
I would imagine most of
the people in those rooms

00:25:12.064 --> 00:25:13.530
are in some shape or form touched

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:15.113
by mental health challenges,

00:25:15.113 --> 00:25:18.200
but does that mean that
we engage in it or not?

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:21.030
And so I'm curious as to
how, as an organization

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:23.488
and Angelina as you how
you've tried to address

00:25:23.488 --> 00:25:24.543
that piece of it too,

00:25:24.543 --> 00:25:27.957
because a lot of times it's
not far from where people

00:25:27.957 --> 00:25:30.870
live in a metaphorical sense,

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:33.667
but in some ways it there's
this aspect of denial

00:25:33.667 --> 00:25:35.493
and not engaging.
- Yes.

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:39.743
- Yeah. I I'll just, I
mean, one of the things,

00:25:39.743 --> 00:25:42.680
I just think it's not only everyday people

00:25:42.680 --> 00:25:44.640
and policy makers are not engaged.

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:47.350
We even have healthcare providers
that don't even know how

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:48.200
to ask these questions.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:50.754
We have, one in five
women who are pregnant,

00:25:50.754 --> 00:25:54.434
who never get asked for
screening for depression

00:25:54.434 --> 00:25:59.434
like Angelina and even even
more that don't get asked,

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:06.979
during whether or not they're
having any kinds of signs

00:26:06.979 --> 00:26:11.705
or symptoms of depression
in the postpartum phase.

00:26:11.705 --> 00:26:14.380
When we look at maternal deaths overall,

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:17.647
about one in nine maternal
deaths are actually attributed to

00:26:17.647 --> 00:26:19.771
issues around maternal health.

00:26:19.771 --> 00:26:21.697
So these issues are really life and death.

00:26:21.697 --> 00:26:24.131
And so part of what we've been saying is,

00:26:24.131 --> 00:26:27.850
"We need to make sure
that everyone is aware

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:30.600
of where these issues show up.

00:26:30.600 --> 00:26:33.440
We need to have everyone
prepared to see certain warning

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:37.011
signs or symptoms that could
help someone get referred to

00:26:37.011 --> 00:26:39.130
help the health they need,"

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:41.568
but we have to have our own
healthcare professionals

00:26:41.568 --> 00:26:44.589
and the health and hospitals
and healthcare providers

00:26:44.589 --> 00:26:46.910
absolutely focused on this

00:26:46.910 --> 00:26:50.690
and not willing to turn a blind
eye to anxiety and stress,

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:52.450
or just like chalking it up as, "Oh yeah.

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:53.283
Well, that's normal.

00:26:53.283 --> 00:26:54.650
There's always a little
postpartum depression."

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:56.597
Well, why is, why should...

00:26:56.597 --> 00:27:00.622
Even if that exists and
we do see it, what owners,

00:27:00.622 --> 00:27:02.097
or what responsibility do you have as

00:27:02.097 --> 00:27:04.350
a healthcare professional
to screen for it,

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:07.623
to refer women to the kinds
of support that they may need,

00:27:07.623 --> 00:27:09.030
ask the question?

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:10.655
And what we're really saying is,

00:27:10.655 --> 00:27:12.420
some of those healthcare professionals,

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:15.147
aren't even the OBGYN where
women may not be even going back

00:27:15.147 --> 00:27:18.390
in for their followup visits
after the baby is born.

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:21.210
Sometimes it's the
pediatricians who need to be,

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:22.796
and we're really glad
pediatricians have this

00:27:22.796 --> 00:27:24.590
on their radar screen increasingly.

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:27.552
So it could be screening
for during well-child visits

00:27:27.552 --> 00:27:28.670
and things like that.

00:27:28.670 --> 00:27:31.158
So it's really kind of raising awareness

00:27:31.158 --> 00:27:36.158
and making it more of a ongoing
way of practicing medicine.

00:27:36.202 --> 00:27:38.790
But knowing that our
health is not determined by

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:41.080
what happens in that
in the doctor's office,

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:45.763
mostly we have to have family
members, community members,

00:27:45.763 --> 00:27:48.964
clergy, and others, willing
to talk about these issues

00:27:48.964 --> 00:27:52.523
and help our fellow, especially new moms.

00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:56.115
If they see, if they are showing signs

00:27:56.115 --> 00:28:01.080
that they're struggling and
make it available for them

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.370
to acknowledge that they may be struggling

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:04.530
and then find that help.

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:06.528
I think Angelina's story
around that is really powerful

00:28:06.528 --> 00:28:10.358
about, how she eventually got
to the help that she needed,

00:28:10.358 --> 00:28:13.287
but thank God that she was
able to be in the hands of

00:28:13.287 --> 00:28:16.740
a care provider who really
paid attention to her,

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:18.970
listened to her and found
her the care that she needed

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:22.021
and recognized it, that she
actually needed it because,

00:28:22.021 --> 00:28:25.365
we wouldn't want to have seen
a worse outcome for Angelina

00:28:25.365 --> 00:28:28.780
and or any other mother
as a result of just

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:30.580
giving birth to their child. Right.

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:34.530
I mean, it's, no woman should
have to struggle on that way.

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:35.640
- Yeah. It's so important,

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:37.760
needs to be elevated all
the way around, as you said,

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:38.737
society-wise, healthcare-wise,

00:28:38.737 --> 00:28:41.270
and I love how you're tying
in all different aspects,

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:43.010
holistically clergy, community members,

00:28:43.010 --> 00:28:45.100
all we need to be on
the same page together

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:47.840
to really make sure the
conversation doesn't get left.

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:50.039
Angelina I wonder if you'd
be able to share a little bit

00:28:50.039 --> 00:28:52.132
about what you've learned
in your journey too,

00:28:52.132 --> 00:28:55.290
and how that's informing the
work that you're doing now,

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:56.900
given as you're on tour.
- Which journey?

00:28:56.900 --> 00:28:58.842
I'm on a lot of journeys.
- That's true.

00:28:58.842 --> 00:29:01.940
The journey with you with your postpartum

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:04.030
after the birth of your new child.

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:07.392
- Oh my gosh. Well, I
feel like I should give

00:29:07.392 --> 00:29:09.514
an abbreviated version of what happened.

00:29:09.514 --> 00:29:10.636
- Because-
- Yeah, if you're willing.

00:29:10.636 --> 00:29:11.501
That be great.

00:29:11.501 --> 00:29:14.200
- No, I mean, I tell my
business for a living so

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:16.123
let's have at it.

00:29:16.123 --> 00:29:18.555
Yeah. So I am married.

00:29:18.555 --> 00:29:22.517
I like to tell people that
because they think that,

00:29:22.517 --> 00:29:25.600
black women don't get
married, but I'm married.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:28.490
I actually planned my daughter.

00:29:28.490 --> 00:29:31.370
That's another stereo type of stigma.

00:29:31.370 --> 00:29:36.370
So married, planned pregnancy,
educated, middle-class

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:39.820
and again, I'm a planner.

00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:41.401
So I plan for this life,

00:29:41.401 --> 00:29:45.283
but I didn't on developing
postpartum depression.

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:50.784
I had a doctor who I felt
like really minimized

00:29:50.784 --> 00:29:54.910
my anxiety during my pregnancy.

00:29:54.910 --> 00:29:56.380
And it was a developing anxiety

00:29:56.380 --> 00:30:00.021
because I was marked high
risk towards the end.

00:30:00.021 --> 00:30:04.589
And I didn't recognize it as
anxiety because I didn't know

00:30:04.589 --> 00:30:07.520
what I was really experiencing in it.

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:09.890
And I didn't know that it had a name.

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:13.462
So, and there was so much preparation

00:30:13.462 --> 00:30:18.462
for the birth of my daughter
that I wasn't really put.

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:21.880
I didn't feel like I was
put on the right track

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:26.203
to be prepared for once
she actually got here.

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:32.150
So when I pushed her out,
there's a picture of me

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:37.280
that's gonna be in the film
where I just looked afraid.

00:30:37.705 --> 00:30:39.050
I look scared.

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:41.490
And that was the first
time they laid her on me.

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:43.370
It was the first time
I looked into her eyes

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:48.120
and I was overwhelmed by
my new responsibilities

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:51.023
as her provider and protector.

00:30:51.023 --> 00:30:52.727
I just, I was scared.

00:30:52.727 --> 00:30:56.433
And then, compound that
with the sleepless nights,

00:30:57.410 --> 00:31:02.410
with the aching body
parts, the diapers, the,

00:31:03.825 --> 00:31:06.730
my relationship with my husband was tested

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:09.791
like all relationships are,
after you have children.

00:31:09.791 --> 00:31:12.471
I just felt like everyone lied to me.

00:31:12.471 --> 00:31:16.395
I felt like my doctors
lied by not preparing me

00:31:16.395 --> 00:31:18.194
for what was to come.

00:31:18.194 --> 00:31:21.074
I felt like my friends, my peers,

00:31:21.074 --> 00:31:25.821
no one was honest about what
their bodies had gone through.

00:31:25.821 --> 00:31:29.230
I just felt like everyone
was kind of muscling through

00:31:29.230 --> 00:31:32.751
and forgetting, forgetting to tell me.

00:31:32.751 --> 00:31:35.980
So when I felt like I was having,

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:39.640
experiencing depressive
thoughts and intrusive thoughts,

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:41.440
which I didn't know was a thing,

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:46.320
now, luckily I was under the
care of a therapist for years.

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:49.650
I had seen my therapist before I,

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:52.235
like six years before I
decided to have my daughter.

00:31:52.235 --> 00:31:54.250
And so she knew my baseline.

00:31:54.250 --> 00:31:55.890
We had a very good relationship

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:59.110
and she was suggesting
things to me over the months

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:01.654
that I was literally
trying to climb myself out.

00:32:01.654 --> 00:32:04.810
And one day at eight months postpartum,

00:32:04.810 --> 00:32:06.940
I went to her office
and she recommended that

00:32:06.940 --> 00:32:09.320
I check into a psychiatric facility.

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:14.320
And most people would be
afraid. I was excited to go.

00:32:15.265 --> 00:32:17.410
I was relieved.

00:32:17.410 --> 00:32:18.432
- I was-
- It was time.

00:32:18.432 --> 00:32:19.416
- It was time.

00:32:19.416 --> 00:32:23.366
It was time to feel better.
I wanted to feel better.

00:32:23.366 --> 00:32:25.508
I just didn't know how.

00:32:25.508 --> 00:32:30.508
And my therapist gave me
permission, to go to this place

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.600
and get, be taken care of.

00:32:34.600 --> 00:32:37.409
And, all my meals were provided.

00:32:37.409 --> 00:32:39.895
It was a great time out,

00:32:39.895 --> 00:32:43.377
that I didn't know that
I could get as a new mom.

00:32:43.377 --> 00:32:45.056
And then since then,

00:32:45.056 --> 00:32:49.120
I've just really been advocating
for new moms, new families,

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:53.525
new parents, and even women
who are yet to become parents.

00:32:53.525 --> 00:32:57.510
I feel like we need to
be honest with them about

00:32:57.510 --> 00:32:58.860
what this is really gonna look like.

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:00.840
- Yep. Yep.
- Cause that ain't

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:02.750
what you think is gonna look like,

00:33:02.750 --> 00:33:04.960
it's not gonna be horrible, it's not bad,

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:06.150
but it's not what you go,

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:07.792
what you think is gonna look like.

00:33:07.792 --> 00:33:12.792
So, and to your former
point about the pandemic

00:33:12.830 --> 00:33:16.760
and the stressors of the pandemic,

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:21.760
I know for myself, the
lockdown, the quarantine made me

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:29.050
and homeschooling made me feel triggered.

00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:33.130
I felt extremely panicked and worried,

00:33:33.130 --> 00:33:38.130
and I felt incapable, all over
again in capable of mothering

00:33:39.641 --> 00:33:42.031
and in capable of balancing.

00:33:42.031 --> 00:33:47.031
And my husband and I
decided after a few months

00:33:47.110 --> 00:33:50.500
that it was time for us to send
our daughter back to daycare

00:33:50.500 --> 00:33:52.330
because I just wasn't able to manage,

00:33:52.330 --> 00:33:56.247
but that was the best decision
for our family at the time.

00:33:56.247 --> 00:34:00.090
And I needed the freedom to
be able to say, "I'm drowning.

00:34:00.090 --> 00:34:01.532
I can't do it."

00:34:01.532 --> 00:34:04.935
But there were a lot of moms who felt,

00:34:04.935 --> 00:34:07.957
the same symptoms of having
postpartum depression

00:34:07.957 --> 00:34:09.691
- Yeah.
- during the pandemic.

00:34:09.691 --> 00:34:11.053
- Yeah. There's a lot there.

00:34:11.053 --> 00:34:13.550
And I appreciate you
sharing that story as well.

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:16.380
I mean, so many pieces that
you're paying it forward

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:17.213
and in a sense, too,

00:34:17.213 --> 00:34:18.890
because even as you were
talking to your story,

00:34:18.890 --> 00:34:20.707
talking about how you
didn't have the language

00:34:20.707 --> 00:34:23.500
to know exactly what
you were going through,

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:25.920
even as someone who had
already been in therapy.

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:28.490
So I think that's a
really critical point too,

00:34:28.490 --> 00:34:30.370
to make sure that people,
as you've already done

00:34:30.370 --> 00:34:32.888
are having those
conversations ahead of time

00:34:32.888 --> 00:34:37.630
and know what to expect
and how to move through it.

00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:38.463
Because even as,

00:34:38.463 --> 00:34:40.430
I mean, both sides are
working simultaneously,

00:34:40.430 --> 00:34:41.289
even as you all are doing,

00:34:41.289 --> 00:34:43.053
trying to make sure that
healthcare workers are providing

00:34:43.053 --> 00:34:45.000
that information,
- Yes.

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:45.843
- having those who are going through it,

00:34:45.843 --> 00:34:47.370
have that information as well.

00:34:47.370 --> 00:34:50.834
And so when the misses still
happen to be able to tackle it

00:34:50.834 --> 00:34:54.760
from both sides, in a sense,
I think is hugely important.

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:55.790
- I think-
- And then everything

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:56.623
you mentioned...

00:34:56.623 --> 00:34:57.456
Oh, go ahead, Stacey.

00:34:57.456 --> 00:34:58.733
- Well one thing I want to say about,

00:34:58.733 --> 00:35:03.440
what Angelina said is that I
think that idea that when you,

00:35:03.440 --> 00:35:04.273
so for a lot of, I mean,

00:35:04.273 --> 00:35:05.370
I'll just speak to my own experience.

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:06.670
Cause it's, I mean,

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:08.710
I didn't go through the level
of postpartum depression

00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:10.045
that Angelina experienced,

00:35:10.045 --> 00:35:12.497
but I do think that there is something

00:35:12.497 --> 00:35:17.422
about becoming a new mother
that is incredibly shocking.

00:35:17.422 --> 00:35:22.422
And like, I don't think anyone is ever...

00:35:22.901 --> 00:35:25.730
I can't imagine that anyone is, I mean,

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:28.680
if you're well-prepared for
motherhood, as soon as it hits,

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:29.820
like God bless you. Right?

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.464
Cause I think it is such an out-of-body,

00:35:32.464 --> 00:35:35.348
like literally having an
out-of-body experience.

00:35:35.348 --> 00:35:37.510
And it's...

00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:39.700
I can remember feeling like the same thing

00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:41.790
when Angelina said, which
is like, "They lied to me."

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:45.350
Because, everyone says, "Oh,
motherhood is so beautiful

00:35:45.350 --> 00:35:47.420
and wonderful and you're gonna love it."

00:35:47.420 --> 00:35:52.030
And honestly, the first few days,

00:35:52.030 --> 00:35:56.320
weeks are so incredibly hard.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:57.570
It is a hard...

00:35:57.570 --> 00:36:02.570
And this is for someone like
me who didn't have to deal with

00:36:02.980 --> 00:36:05.760
as many of the mental health challenges.

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:08.846
It didn't matter. It was
incredibly difficult.

00:36:08.846 --> 00:36:12.020
And I was someone who had already, like,

00:36:12.020 --> 00:36:13.800
I had my kids really late.

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:17.071
I mean, my first daughter
was born when I was 38.

00:36:17.071 --> 00:36:19.700
And I, by then I'd been at the workplace,

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:22.570
work world and I had done all
these things in my career.

00:36:22.570 --> 00:36:26.490
And so to be home for four
months at home, by myself,

00:36:26.490 --> 00:36:28.890
with my child who cannot speak to me,

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:30.713
I could not have a cogent conversation

00:36:30.713 --> 00:36:32.360
(all laughing)
with this little child,

00:36:32.360 --> 00:36:34.544
- Yeah.
- and then, it was like,

00:36:34.544 --> 00:36:37.600
I was like, I don't
think I'll survive this.

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:39.290
This is so hard.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:41.310
So I think, and then of course,

00:36:41.310 --> 00:36:45.540
all of your physiological
changes are complicating

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:48.520
how you process all this,
all the lack of sleep,

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:52.009
trying to eat healthy,
eat at all first of all.

00:36:52.009 --> 00:36:54.880
(all laughing)
I mean, it's just like,

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:57.777
all sort of combined with each
other, into like a whole...

00:36:57.777 --> 00:37:00.681
And then you get for a lot of women,

00:37:00.681 --> 00:37:03.817
you get past the first three
months or for six months

00:37:03.817 --> 00:37:06.002
or whatever and things start to kind of,

00:37:06.002 --> 00:37:07.950
they're still difficult
and you're not getting,

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:11.170
still not getting sleep, but
it starts to kind of settle in.

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:15.120
But I just think we've got to
do a different, a better job,

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:19.990
helping women prepare for
motherhood in a very realistic way

00:37:19.990 --> 00:37:23.090
so that things like Angelina
where, you don't get,

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:25.550
feel like you get hit by a two
by four, when a baby comes,

00:37:25.550 --> 00:37:27.482
you actually are prepared to say, "Okay,

00:37:27.482 --> 00:37:28.968
this is gonna be the hardest thing

00:37:28.968 --> 00:37:30.330
you've ever done in your life.

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:32.775
And it's gonna start second one,

00:37:32.775 --> 00:37:34.687
when that baby comes,
- Yes!

00:37:34.687 --> 00:37:37.688
- in the hospital, when
that baby's on your chest.

00:37:37.688 --> 00:37:39.870
- You're like, Whoa! Okay!
- Yeah.

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:42.245
- I mean, you need a plan, absolutely.

00:37:42.245 --> 00:37:45.841
- (laughing) Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:37:45.841 --> 00:37:48.380
- I think that's so important
that you're verbalizing,

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:51.120
vocalizing that too so
people aren't surprised,

00:37:51.120 --> 00:37:52.890
even though I'm sure,
I mean, there's no way

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:53.769
and obviously I'm not speaking
- Right!

00:37:53.769 --> 00:37:54.602
- from experience,
- There's no way.

00:37:54.602 --> 00:37:56.678
- no way to prepare completely
- 100%.

00:37:56.678 --> 00:37:58.540
- But at least they have, the expectation

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:00.650
that you won't be able
to prepare completely.

00:38:00.650 --> 00:38:01.504
- Yeah.
- And that

00:38:01.504 --> 00:38:06.504
there are somethings that
you can do to set yourself up

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:08.660
for some level of success.

00:38:08.660 --> 00:38:10.988
And for me, had I known the information,

00:38:10.988 --> 00:38:12.820
I would have planned differently.

00:38:12.820 --> 00:38:15.130
I would have hired a
postpartum doula, right.

00:38:15.130 --> 00:38:18.978
I would've, I would have
made sure that my husband,

00:38:18.978 --> 00:38:23.978
banked vacation days so that
he stayed home, longer with me.

00:38:24.639 --> 00:38:27.950
Honestly, I would have taken a second job,

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:31.844
to pay for childcare because
that's what, I needed relief.

00:38:31.844 --> 00:38:35.730
So going in knowing what you can handle

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:38.533
and what resources you can call on,

00:38:38.533 --> 00:38:41.187
whatever it takes, you will,

00:38:41.187 --> 00:38:44.687
you can at least engage the plan

00:38:44.687 --> 00:38:46.940
so that you're not so overwhelmed.

00:38:46.940 --> 00:38:50.057
And so disengaged from reality.

00:38:50.057 --> 00:38:51.403
- It's also like, I mean,

00:38:51.403 --> 00:38:53.261
this is where we look at the U.S

00:38:53.261 --> 00:38:55.618
and compare ourselves to
other countries, right?

00:38:55.618 --> 00:38:58.069
In other countries, you do have midwives

00:38:58.069 --> 00:39:03.069
and why don't we have
a whole program where

00:39:04.496 --> 00:39:07.520
everyone irrespective
of income or anything

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:11.309
can access a doula during
pregnancy and childbirth

00:39:11.309 --> 00:39:12.677
and even postpartum,

00:39:12.677 --> 00:39:15.281
why don't we have the resources
in this country to pay for

00:39:15.281 --> 00:39:18.160
that and make that available
to any woman that may need it?

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:20.750
Why don't we have paid
family leave in this country,

00:39:20.750 --> 00:39:23.532
which is pretty standard
in other countries, right?

00:39:23.532 --> 00:39:26.073
Why don't we provide,

00:39:26.073 --> 00:39:30.505
nursing and home visit visitation the way,

00:39:30.505 --> 00:39:32.427
it exists in other countries?

00:39:32.427 --> 00:39:34.810
Why don't we have universal coverage,

00:39:34.810 --> 00:39:37.970
so if women are experiencing,
mental health challenges,

00:39:37.970 --> 00:39:39.250
they have the resources, I mean like,

00:39:39.250 --> 00:39:43.830
there are so many levels of
where we have under-invested

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:48.227
in our human capital, that
show up in all of the things

00:39:48.227 --> 00:39:51.542
that Angelina talks about in
her own story that we see in

00:39:51.542 --> 00:39:54.469
the statistics around
maternal and infant health.

00:39:54.469 --> 00:39:57.020
And frankly, I'm not
saying writing a check

00:39:57.020 --> 00:39:59.470
and putting more money in these
things actually eliminates

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:01.760
the problem because then
we have the other issue,

00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:04.254
especially as it relates
to black and brown women,

00:40:04.254 --> 00:40:08.577
where just the basic effects
of racism and discrimination

00:40:08.577 --> 00:40:10.766
impact us on a daily basis.

00:40:10.766 --> 00:40:14.460
And that skews the outcomes
as well, disproportionately

00:40:14.460 --> 00:40:17.140
relative to women that
look like me and Angelina,

00:40:17.140 --> 00:40:19.486
but having said that, a lot of this,

00:40:19.486 --> 00:40:22.828
and when you look at ways in
which to address anti-racism

00:40:22.828 --> 00:40:27.204
and bias in this country,
policy solutions are at the core

00:40:27.204 --> 00:40:31.072
of what we could start to do
to reverse some of the impacts

00:40:31.072 --> 00:40:33.450
of what we've been
experiencing over generations

00:40:33.450 --> 00:40:35.420
and that we're still living with.

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:38.414
And frankly, to be in
a country, this wealthy

00:40:38.414 --> 00:40:43.414
and to not being provided the
basic bare minimum for women

00:40:44.526 --> 00:40:47.440
as they're bringing a
new life in the world,

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:49.580
is just simply unacceptable.

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:50.413
I mean, it does...

00:40:50.413 --> 00:40:53.950
And it's why I think both of
us are so passionate around,

00:40:53.950 --> 00:40:57.197
like we can't live in
this world and society

00:40:57.197 --> 00:40:58.622
and tolerate this

00:40:58.622 --> 00:41:01.970
and we shouldn't tolerate
for any like the term.

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:03.260
- Yeah, very well said.

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:04.590
And I think it's the support,

00:41:04.590 --> 00:41:06.302
the support isn't there, I
think that's what Angelina

00:41:06.302 --> 00:41:08.130
was referring to and what,
- Yeah exactly and-

00:41:08.130 --> 00:41:09.480
you're articulate as well, just,

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:12.112
it's so critical, but still lacking.

00:41:12.112 --> 00:41:14.313
- We need the policy support.

00:41:14.313 --> 00:41:17.535
That's a huge component
of the work I do also,

00:41:17.535 --> 00:41:21.400
is like, moms in California,

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:23.370
I worked on legislation there

00:41:23.370 --> 00:41:26.142
to help with expanding screenings

00:41:26.142 --> 00:41:28.603
and also training physicians,

00:41:28.603 --> 00:41:32.525
because as Stacey mentioned, doctors,

00:41:32.525 --> 00:41:36.130
and labor and delivery
nurses and folks in L and D,

00:41:36.130 --> 00:41:38.010
just in general, may not know,

00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:40.755
how to implement these
trainings or these screenings.

00:41:40.755 --> 00:41:44.534
So there needs to be resources
and time that's invested

00:41:44.534 --> 00:41:48.430
to make sure that they're
able to now do this work.

00:41:48.430 --> 00:41:51.170
And also when red flags are raised,

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:53.550
where do we send these moms?

00:41:53.550 --> 00:41:58.470
I was reaching out to my OBGYN at

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:00.400
two weeks after my eight week checkup.

00:42:00.400 --> 00:42:03.830
Like, "Hey, I need more
resources. I need help.

00:42:03.830 --> 00:42:07.450
I need medication. I need a therapist."

00:42:07.450 --> 00:42:09.807
And she was just kind of
like a deer in headlights.

00:42:09.807 --> 00:42:11.550
- Oh boy.
- Yeah.

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:14.920
And then she prescribed
the medication to me,

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:16.510
but never followed up.

00:42:16.510 --> 00:42:20.410
So, there's more work to be done,

00:42:20.410 --> 00:42:22.350
especially on the policy level.

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:24.450
So that's a part of the reason why I did

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:26.480
the post-partum revolution trip,

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:30.360
was so that we could advocate
for the same, for moms

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:33.110
to have the same exact
treatment in California,

00:42:33.110 --> 00:42:34.520
all over the country.

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:38.640
There's no reason why moms in
North Dakota or in Tennessee

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:41.200
where the reproductive
rights are in jeopardy

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:45.840
and are constantly up for
debate and on the ballot box,

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:50.130
but there's no reinforcement
or there's no help once

00:42:50.130 --> 00:42:52.700
the babies are here for mom.
- Yeah.

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:55.293
It's one of the big reasons
why one of the things

00:42:55.293 --> 00:42:58.010
that we advocate for,

00:42:58.010 --> 00:43:00.940
and we're pushing for the
federal level is mom the bus,

00:43:00.940 --> 00:43:03.460
which is a whole bunch
of legislative actions

00:43:03.460 --> 00:43:05.280
that would address all these things

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:08.510
from maternal mental health
to, all the issues of

00:43:08.510 --> 00:43:10.130
reshaping the perinatal workforce.

00:43:10.130 --> 00:43:11.960
So we can have more doulas and midwives

00:43:11.960 --> 00:43:15.440
and a whole range of other other aspects.

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:17.720
The other thing that March
of Dimes we've done is like

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:20.450
introduce a lot of programs to
provide additional levels of

00:43:20.450 --> 00:43:22.490
support, so for example, we,

00:43:22.490 --> 00:43:27.330
and we even transferred
to a virtual program

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:29.680
around supportive pregnancy care,

00:43:29.680 --> 00:43:30.920
really group prenatal care,

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:34.950
which really allows women to
go through their prenatal care,

00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:37.420
obviously checking in
with their physician,

00:43:37.420 --> 00:43:39.440
but also checking in with other moms

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:41.460
that are pregnant along with them.

00:43:41.460 --> 00:43:44.280
So that you can have an honest
conversation with other women

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:46.450
that might experiencing
some of the same things.

00:43:46.450 --> 00:43:48.810
And then continuing that
kind of group care into

00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:50.270
the postpartum phase.

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:52.970
Because just when, I mean,
when the baby comes is just

00:43:54.064 --> 00:43:55.220
the beginning of your problems, right?

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:57.650
It's like now, you have a
whole set of other issues

00:43:57.650 --> 00:44:00.780
to deal with and you
still need that support,

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:03.970
postpartum as well and the
last thing I have to say too,

00:44:03.970 --> 00:44:07.020
is going back to this issue
of how the healthcare system

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:09.470
needs to acknowledge the unique challenges

00:44:09.470 --> 00:44:12.237
that black and brown women
face and how we know that

00:44:12.237 --> 00:44:14.640
the healthcare system
does not listen to us

00:44:14.640 --> 00:44:17.550
and respond to us in
the ways that we need.

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:20.320
We introduced an implicit
bias training program

00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:22.340
for healthcare professionals
about two years ago,

00:44:22.340 --> 00:44:25.570
we've trained 15,000
healthcare professionals

00:44:25.570 --> 00:44:27.630
to recognize their implicit bias.

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:30.290
And now we have a new
component which starts

00:44:30.290 --> 00:44:32.290
to go through different scenarios,

00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:34.660
so you can play out
what scenarios look like

00:44:34.660 --> 00:44:37.820
to see where your implicit
bias actually does show up

00:44:37.820 --> 00:44:40.610
in your day to day interactions
with your patients.

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:43.050
So, and not just doctors and nurses,

00:44:43.050 --> 00:44:46.840
it's really even like the front
desk receptionist who like

00:44:46.840 --> 00:44:49.720
give you a side eye when you
show your Medicaid card. Right.

00:44:49.720 --> 00:44:52.060
Which is like all of a sudden
now I don't wanna come back

00:44:52.060 --> 00:44:54.990
to this clinic because I don't
feel, I don't feel valued.

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:57.420
I don't, I feel like I
feel diminished, right.

00:44:57.420 --> 00:44:59.779
And that happens far too often.

00:44:59.779 --> 00:45:02.417
And so there all these things we need,

00:45:02.417 --> 00:45:05.040
but we just have to do this at scale.

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:07.540
If we're gonna see these
kinds of outcomes improve in

00:45:07.540 --> 00:45:09.860
our lifetime.
- Yeah wow.

00:45:09.860 --> 00:45:11.320
That's really encouraging
to hear all the steps

00:45:11.320 --> 00:45:13.370
that you all have taken across the board,

00:45:13.370 --> 00:45:15.700
because it's a huge undertaking.

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:18.750
I mean, you're dealing with
a system that's already

00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:19.583
at so many levels,

00:45:19.583 --> 00:45:21.310
even as you mentioned from
receptionist all the way up,

00:45:21.310 --> 00:45:23.110
there's going to be so
many biases along the way.

00:45:23.110 --> 00:45:26.400
And each step along the way,
it's gonna have an impact so.

00:45:26.400 --> 00:45:27.588
- Yes.
- Even as I know,

00:45:27.588 --> 00:45:31.300
the time is going fast, which
is unfortunate in a sense,

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:33.840
but I did wanna kind of
tie it back to this idea of

00:45:33.840 --> 00:45:35.573
the strong black woman,
cause you've talked about,

00:45:35.573 --> 00:45:37.640
the things that you've
done on the policy side

00:45:37.640 --> 00:45:39.900
and things like that, but
I'm also curious just how,

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:41.410
as you're working in different communities

00:45:41.410 --> 00:45:43.230
and in communities of
color, in particular,

00:45:43.230 --> 00:45:45.800
how people are embracing of this,

00:45:45.800 --> 00:45:48.000
this need for support along the way,

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:50.157
or is there still this tension of,

00:45:50.157 --> 00:45:51.180
"Well as strong black women,

00:45:51.180 --> 00:45:53.010
we don't need to tap into this."

00:45:53.010 --> 00:45:56.620
I'm just curious how that,
how that conversation going.

00:45:56.620 --> 00:45:58.080
I'm gonna go to Angelina first,

00:45:58.080 --> 00:46:02.420
since I see you smiling as
I'm making those comments.

00:46:02.420 --> 00:46:05.310
- Well, Stacey has seen my comedy special

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:07.220
that we are touring
around the country with.

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:11.280
I have a whole section, that addresses

00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:14.860
the strong black woman,
(all laughing)

00:46:14.860 --> 00:46:18.830
because that myth, we've
gotta flip that on its head.

00:46:18.830 --> 00:46:21.810
Strength is asking for help.

00:46:21.810 --> 00:46:24.130
Strength is saving the money,

00:46:24.130 --> 00:46:27.193
so that you can pay the help, guilt-free,

00:46:28.540 --> 00:46:32.860
strength is employing people
to make your life easier.

00:46:32.860 --> 00:46:36.390
Strength is enjoying
luxury as black women.

00:46:36.390 --> 00:46:39.530
We are because we don't have a history,

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:42.550
of indulging in these kinds of things,

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:46.530
we don't have, when we
are suddenly able to,

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:51.530
we kind of had this guilt or
there's shame and judgment

00:46:52.670 --> 00:46:55.260
from family, from friends.

00:46:55.260 --> 00:46:56.880
There's a whole thing behind it.

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:59.870
But I always tell people,

00:46:59.870 --> 00:47:02.730
strength is really, truly in asking

00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:05.833
and allowing people to help you.

00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:12.390
My problem was was that I
didn't know what to ask for,

00:47:12.390 --> 00:47:15.420
to be helped, and that's
where the preparation for me

00:47:15.420 --> 00:47:18.480
would have come in, had
I known what to expect,

00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:21.450
I would have been prepared
with what to ask for,

00:47:21.450 --> 00:47:24.980
but then also allowing people to help you.

00:47:24.980 --> 00:47:28.560
I allowed my therapist to
help me. That was her job.

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:30.740
I allowed my mom to help me,

00:47:30.740 --> 00:47:33.450
but I didn't allow my cousins

00:47:33.450 --> 00:47:36.080
and my other supportive
family members to help,

00:47:36.080 --> 00:47:38.470
until I was in crisis.

00:47:38.470 --> 00:47:42.380
And that's what I know,
moving forward that

00:47:42.380 --> 00:47:46.150
I tell black women and
mothers, mothers in general,

00:47:46.150 --> 00:47:48.533
we feel like we should
be able to do it all.

00:47:49.600 --> 00:47:51.760
Carry a baby in the
front, one in the back,

00:47:51.760 --> 00:47:55.500
push one in the stroller,
answer, work emails.

00:47:55.500 --> 00:47:58.380
On a call, on a Zoom, all at the same....

00:47:58.380 --> 00:48:00.020
Mama, we ain't got to do all that.

00:48:00.020 --> 00:48:01.310
No, ma'am no, ma'am no ma'am.

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:02.470
We need to have a seat.

00:48:02.470 --> 00:48:05.340
But the strong black woman,

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:07.350
the strong black woman asks for help.

00:48:07.350 --> 00:48:09.330
So sisters let's start asking for help

00:48:09.330 --> 00:48:11.020
and receiving the help.
- Yes.

00:48:11.020 --> 00:48:12.582
- That's so good.

00:48:12.582 --> 00:48:17.120
- There's a question
black women have been,

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:22.120
have had to be incredibly resilient over

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:24.300
many, many generations.

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:28.480
And it's funny because I go back to

00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:32.560
the book about leaning in,
remember Sheryl Sandberg.

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:35.143
And I remember thinking how absurd,

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:38.490
the idea that have the choice to lean

00:48:38.490 --> 00:48:39.940
and should I lean in or not?

00:48:39.940 --> 00:48:40.790
It's like, "Well,

00:48:40.790 --> 00:48:43.363
black women haven't actually
had that choice." Right?

00:48:43.363 --> 00:48:45.530
(all laughing)
- True.

00:48:45.530 --> 00:48:47.800
- Like we only have a
choice, but the lean in like,

00:48:47.800 --> 00:48:48.993
like our families are depending on us.

00:48:48.993 --> 00:48:52.099
It's like, the world falls
apart, iff we fall apart,

00:48:52.099 --> 00:48:55.150
like it's actually,
that's how it is. Right.

00:48:55.150 --> 00:49:00.150
And so I think we have to, I
think we can be both proud of,

00:49:01.670 --> 00:49:04.530
that we have proven time and time again,

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:06.920
that we are always ready and willing

00:49:06.920 --> 00:49:10.400
to rise to the challenge
and to do what is necessary.

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:12.870
But we have to also acknowledge
that that has traditionally

00:49:12.870 --> 00:49:16.300
come at an extraordinary
cost to our health

00:49:16.300 --> 00:49:18.410
and to our both mental
health and physical health.

00:49:18.410 --> 00:49:20.530
There was research done on, on weathering,

00:49:20.530 --> 00:49:21.470
the idea of weathering,

00:49:21.470 --> 00:49:26.470
which is kind of how our
health deteriorates over time,

00:49:26.580 --> 00:49:28.920
because of just the lived
experience of being a black woman

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:30.010
in this country.

00:49:30.010 --> 00:49:32.270
And that weathering then
has an impact on our birth,

00:49:32.270 --> 00:49:34.210
on birth outcomes, around babies,

00:49:34.210 --> 00:49:37.510
but that stress and
anxiety layered on layered,

00:49:37.510 --> 00:49:40.240
year after year, generation
after generation,

00:49:40.240 --> 00:49:44.090
actually deteriorates our
lives and our physical health.

00:49:44.090 --> 00:49:46.770
And that is not okay.

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:48.500
It is not okay for us to live in

00:49:49.580 --> 00:49:52.770
a way where we're
constantly on adrenaline,

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:54.680
constantly on fight or flight,

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:58.240
because that eventually wears
us down to the point where we

00:49:58.240 --> 00:49:59.823
see all these chronic health conditions.

00:49:59.823 --> 00:50:03.140
I mean, I've told this story
long, have many stories.

00:50:03.140 --> 00:50:05.480
Angela has heard me say this
about my own experience of

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:07.610
growing up with my dad,
who was a physician

00:50:07.610 --> 00:50:09.790
and him having all these
patients that have stress

00:50:09.790 --> 00:50:14.730
and anxiety, but what they
also had was chronic obesity,

00:50:14.730 --> 00:50:17.430
hypertension, diabetes,

00:50:17.430 --> 00:50:22.430
but he often mostly diagnosed
them with anxiety first, like,

00:50:23.270 --> 00:50:26.150
and this was in the 60, 50, 60, 70s.

00:50:26.150 --> 00:50:29.980
Like he, even it and
recognized and although he,

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:31.633
I don't know that there
was as much research out at

00:50:31.633 --> 00:50:34.770
that time, but he was
actually documenting,

00:50:34.770 --> 00:50:36.650
the connection between stress and anxiety

00:50:36.650 --> 00:50:38.701
and this other chronic health conditions.

00:50:38.701 --> 00:50:40.700
And I carried that thought,

00:50:40.700 --> 00:50:42.850
I have carried that thought
with me my entire life,

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:46.060
until I walked into the
opposites of Marsh labs,

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:47.400
became the President CEO

00:50:47.400 --> 00:50:50.130
and started to see the actual research

00:50:50.130 --> 00:50:52.820
to the evidence around this.

00:50:52.820 --> 00:50:55.270
So here, here we are today at all.

00:50:55.270 --> 00:50:57.723
All we got, all we can
do right now is to say,

00:50:59.070 --> 00:51:02.850
one, we have got to make sure that women,

00:51:02.850 --> 00:51:04.710
especially women of color,

00:51:04.710 --> 00:51:07.200
find a place in a space in this country

00:51:07.200 --> 00:51:09.920
and I call out the United
States in particular,

00:51:09.920 --> 00:51:12.470
although we could say
this is a global thing,

00:51:12.470 --> 00:51:17.090
but in this particular country,
we have a unique experience

00:51:17.090 --> 00:51:21.090
of having to still address
the legacy and of slavery

00:51:21.090 --> 00:51:24.230
that has, and we're still
feeling the effects of that,

00:51:24.230 --> 00:51:29.230
today, today, and what we
ought to do in this generation

00:51:29.290 --> 00:51:30.960
that we are the leaders of right now,

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:33.080
is to call attention to it

00:51:33.080 --> 00:51:36.730
and to try to do as much as
we can to reverse the impact,

00:51:36.730 --> 00:51:40.640
so that Ava, who was Angelina's
daughter or my daughters,

00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:43.090
Maddie and Savannah,
do not have to continue

00:51:43.090 --> 00:51:45.500
to live in a world in which
they are continued to be,

00:51:45.500 --> 00:51:49.550
they're subjected to the same level of,

00:51:49.550 --> 00:51:52.630
kind of oppressiveness that
so many black and brown women

00:51:52.630 --> 00:51:53.630
have to face this country.

00:51:53.630 --> 00:51:54.650
There shouldn't, we should,

00:51:54.650 --> 00:51:56.340
we have to do everything we can.

00:51:56.340 --> 00:51:58.300
Fortunately, we're
standing on the shoulders

00:51:58.300 --> 00:52:02.250
of many black women and brown
women who fought very hard.

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:04.010
It is our job to get to you that fight

00:52:04.010 --> 00:52:07.143
and to elevate that fight
elevated, do even more.

00:52:08.773 --> 00:52:10.920
- It's so important and you're
right in terms of the legacy

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:13.370
and just the need to move things forward.

00:52:13.370 --> 00:52:15.210
I mean, it's even encouraging to hear,

00:52:15.210 --> 00:52:18.050
encouraging and sobering to
hear the story about your father

00:52:18.050 --> 00:52:21.470
as well, documenting
those things at that time,

00:52:21.470 --> 00:52:23.720
because as you mentioned that
wasn't highly documented.

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:25.800
I mean, there's more evidence
for that now clearly,

00:52:25.800 --> 00:52:28.447
and just the detrimental effects
of racism on mental health

00:52:28.447 --> 00:52:30.710
and so many other aspects of our health

00:52:30.710 --> 00:52:31.940
and especially for black women.

00:52:31.940 --> 00:52:35.230
So I'm deeply appreciative
to what you're both doing

00:52:35.230 --> 00:52:36.070
in that way.

00:52:36.070 --> 00:52:36.903
And to wrap up,

00:52:36.903 --> 00:52:39.090
I just wanted to also just
hear what gives you hope

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:41.590
in this moment with all the challenges

00:52:41.590 --> 00:52:44.270
that clearly still need to be addressed

00:52:44.270 --> 00:52:48.090
in so many ways around mom
health, mom and baby health,

00:52:48.090 --> 00:52:48.923
mom's mental health,

00:52:48.923 --> 00:52:50.710
but what gives you hope moving forward?

00:52:52.100 --> 00:52:53.450
- Well, I'll start.

00:52:53.450 --> 00:52:58.450
It, what gives me hope is
seeing the impact of the work,

00:53:02.160 --> 00:53:06.577
seeing the impact of an idea that I had,

00:53:10.020 --> 00:53:14.310
or to take a risk and share the fact that

00:53:14.310 --> 00:53:18.440
I was admitted to a psychiatric
facility on social media

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:22.370
and to see moms open up to me

00:53:22.370 --> 00:53:26.524
and ask me to help them check
into a psychiatric facility

00:53:26.524 --> 00:53:28.330
and facilitate that.

00:53:28.330 --> 00:53:30.330
That gives me hope.
- Wow.

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:35.330
- It gives me hope, to
see my daughter Ava,

00:53:35.350 --> 00:53:40.350
and to see that she at six
years old has the language,

00:53:41.960 --> 00:53:44.410
not quite the understanding just yet,

00:53:44.410 --> 00:53:48.420
but she knows the language
of the postpartum revolution,

00:53:48.420 --> 00:53:52.520
postpartum depression, helping
moms, helping families,

00:53:52.520 --> 00:53:56.001
helping babies, that gives me hope.

00:53:56.001 --> 00:53:58.233
There's still a lot more work to do,

00:53:59.160 --> 00:54:01.620
on the postpartum revolution road trip.

00:54:01.620 --> 00:54:06.330
I recently met with Congresswoman
Lisa Blunt Rochester,

00:54:06.330 --> 00:54:10.340
who is she's working on
the mom the bus and she is-

00:54:10.340 --> 00:54:13.290
- She's awesome.
(all laughing)

00:54:13.290 --> 00:54:17.820
- A dynamite woman like Stacey, we,

00:54:17.820 --> 00:54:19.883
I had an immediate connection with.

00:54:20.930 --> 00:54:25.190
I also had a very great
policy meeting with

00:54:25.190 --> 00:54:29.190
Congresswoman Yvette Clark on the hill.

00:54:29.190 --> 00:54:34.190
And both women are super
supportive of my mission

00:54:34.760 --> 00:54:38.410
and my goal to help formulate legislation,

00:54:38.410 --> 00:54:41.920
to support new moms and to work with

00:54:42.770 --> 00:54:44.650
those of us who worked on
the bill in California,

00:54:44.650 --> 00:54:48.010
the bills on California to
make federal legislation.

00:54:48.010 --> 00:54:49.430
That gives me hope.

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:53.490
The fact that these women
are willing to meet with me,

00:54:53.490 --> 00:54:56.007
an average woman who as Hollywood says,

00:54:56.007 --> 00:54:57.970
"No one knows who you are,"

00:54:57.970 --> 00:55:02.970
but these women took the time
to hear me and to meet with me

00:55:03.270 --> 00:55:06.680
and their policy directors so
that we can help moms who were

00:55:06.680 --> 00:55:08.350
in similar situations,

00:55:08.350 --> 00:55:11.680
but also helped moms
avoid similar situations.

00:55:11.680 --> 00:55:13.382
That gives me hope.

00:55:13.382 --> 00:55:14.420
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:55:14.420 --> 00:55:18.252
I think what gives me
hope is just the momentum.

00:55:18.252 --> 00:55:22.010
Again, just seeing how
we're having different

00:55:22.010 --> 00:55:24.950
kinds of conversations, where,

00:55:24.950 --> 00:55:27.433
we weren't having these
conversations before,

00:55:28.470 --> 00:55:31.930
I can see minds being open to

00:55:31.930 --> 00:55:34.110
really understanding these issues.

00:55:34.110 --> 00:55:38.090
I can see people not being
so quick to diminish.

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:40.230
If a woman has a problem
with her pregnancy,

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:41.390
it must be her fault.

00:55:41.390 --> 00:55:43.420
It's not her fault. Right.

00:55:43.420 --> 00:55:44.770
And people are starting
to say, wait a minute,

00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:48.483
maybe there's something more
systemically wrong here.

00:55:49.434 --> 00:55:52.040
And that's where in Washington, I mean,

00:55:52.040 --> 00:55:53.350
that's in both sides of the Aisle.

00:55:53.350 --> 00:55:55.502
I see what Republicans, I mean,

00:55:55.502 --> 00:55:57.240
when I see Republican members of Congress,

00:55:57.240 --> 00:56:00.430
who are like moved, literally
authentically moved to tears,

00:56:00.430 --> 00:56:02.750
when they hear a story
like from Charles Johnson,

00:56:02.750 --> 00:56:05.310
about losing his wife, Kira,

00:56:05.310 --> 00:56:08.250
hours after their second child was born.

00:56:08.250 --> 00:56:10.660
I mean, these are human issues.

00:56:10.660 --> 00:56:14.240
And we're living in a time when

00:56:14.240 --> 00:56:15.950
there's a lot that doesn't make sense.

00:56:15.950 --> 00:56:18.420
And there's a lot of
craziness that goes on.

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:21.190
And there's a lot of things
that people don't believe.

00:56:21.190 --> 00:56:24.340
A lot of facts, people just
don't aren't willing accept.

00:56:24.340 --> 00:56:27.970
And a lot of people that other
people are willing to dismiss

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:31.530
as if their concerns
or issues don't matter.

00:56:31.530 --> 00:56:34.250
And what I'm finding is that
at least on the issue of

00:56:34.250 --> 00:56:35.780
mom and baby health,

00:56:35.780 --> 00:56:40.150
there is the ability to
find some common ground.

00:56:40.150 --> 00:56:43.040
We were at an event last
week with two Republican

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:45.660
members of Congress and
two democratic members.

00:56:45.660 --> 00:56:46.537
And one of them said,

00:56:46.537 --> 00:56:49.760
"I love coming to a March of Dimes event

00:56:49.760 --> 00:56:51.300
because I think this
is the one place where,

00:56:51.300 --> 00:56:53.080
Republicans and Democrats can agree."

00:56:53.080 --> 00:56:54.470
And they weren't talking
about just March of Dimes,

00:56:54.470 --> 00:56:55.343
they were really talking
about the bigger issues

00:56:55.343 --> 00:56:57.330
that we're working on,

00:56:57.330 --> 00:57:00.180
but that is encouraging
and inspiring to me.

00:57:00.180 --> 00:57:03.473
So I think we just gotta keep
that momentum going, right?

00:57:03.473 --> 00:57:05.139
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:57:05.139 --> 00:57:05.972
- So yeah.
- So again,

00:57:05.972 --> 00:57:06.805
I'm deeply appreciated both of you

00:57:06.805 --> 00:57:07.780
for the work that you're doing,

00:57:07.780 --> 00:57:10.270
also for both of you taking
time to come on this podcast.

00:57:10.270 --> 00:57:12.080
I know this is gonna be
something that our listeners are

00:57:12.080 --> 00:57:13.600
gonna really resonate with.

00:57:13.600 --> 00:57:14.433
Any places

00:57:14.433 --> 00:57:15.290
they should go to find out.
- Well thank you

00:57:15.290 --> 00:57:16.510
for doing this too,
you're doing a great job.

00:57:16.510 --> 00:57:18.702
We appreciate that (laughing).
- Oh thank you.

00:57:18.702 --> 00:57:20.070
- It gives hope that you as a black man,

00:57:20.070 --> 00:57:22.503
are giving the sisters, the space ha ha.

00:57:23.793 --> 00:57:24.720
- Of course, it's the east I can do.

00:57:24.720 --> 00:57:29.330
- Not only the space, but the
awareness of your audience

00:57:29.330 --> 00:57:32.470
to value what, to value these issues.

00:57:32.470 --> 00:57:35.170
I'm just like, "Come on. That
gives me hope, black man.

00:57:35.170 --> 00:57:37.130
Come on now."
- It's the least I can do.

00:57:37.130 --> 00:57:39.700
- And here it says a Yale
professor and researcher,

00:57:39.700 --> 00:57:44.220
a man of faith, and a sports
and hip hop fan is our host.

00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:46.509
How about that?
- How about that?

00:57:46.509 --> 00:57:47.940
(all laughing)
A balance.

00:57:47.940 --> 00:57:49.920
- Bringing it full circle.
- Yes.

00:57:49.920 --> 00:57:50.959
- I'm deeply appreciative.

00:57:50.959 --> 00:57:54.040
And again, just to see what
you all do as black women.

00:57:54.040 --> 00:57:56.880
So encouraging the least
